<div class="textbook-container">

    <!-- Card: Introduction -->
    <div class="card">
        <div class="card-header">
            <span class="card-icon">📘</span>
            <h2>Introduction</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>
                Electric field and electric potential are two fundamental ways of describing how electric charges interact. While the electric field describes the <strong>force</strong> a charge experiences, the electric potential describes the <strong>energy</strong> a charge possesses due to its position in the field.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Card: Definition - Electric Field -->
    <div class="card">
        <div class="card-header">
            <span class="card-icon">⚡</span>
            <h2>Definition: Electric Field</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>
                The <strong>electric field</strong> is a region around a charged particle or object within which a force would be exerted on other charged particles or objects. 
                <br><br>
                It is a <span class="tag tag-vector">Vector Quantity</span>, meaning it has both magnitude and direction. By convention, the direction of the electric field is the direction of the force that would be exerted on a <strong>positive</strong> test charge placed in the field.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Card: Formula - Electric Field -->
    <div class="card formula-card">
        <div class="card-header">
            <span class="card-icon">📐</span>
            <h2>Formula: Electric Field</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <div class="formula-display">
                $$ \vec{E} = \frac{\vec{F}}{q} $$
            </div>

            <div class="variable-legend">
                <div class="variable-item"><code>\vec{E}</code> Electric Field Vector</div>
                <div class="variable-item"><code>\vec{F}</code> Electric Force</div>
                <div class="variable-item"><code>q</code> Test Charge</div>
            </div>

            <div class="formula-actions">
                <button class="btn btn-outline" onclick="alert('The electric field is defined as the force per unit charge. If you know the force on a small test charge, dividing by that charge gives the field strength.')">Explain Formula</button>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="alert('Opening Formula Lab: Adjust charge q and Force F to see how E changes.')">Open Formula Lab</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Card: Definition - Electric Potential -->
    <div class="card">
        <div class="card-header">
            <span class="card-icon">🔋</span>
            <h2>Definition: Electric Potential</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <p>
                <strong>Electric potential</strong> (or simply potential) is the amount of work energy needed to move a unit of electric charge from a reference point to a specific point in an electric field. 
                <br><br>
                It is a <span class="tag tag-scalar">Scalar Quantity</span>, meaning it has magnitude but no direction. It is often compared to height in a gravitational field: just as an object has potential energy due to its height, a charge has potential energy due to its electric potential.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Card: Formula - Electric Potential -->
    <div class="card formula-card">
        <div class="card-header">
            <span class="card-icon">📐</span>
            <h2>Formula: Electric Potential</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <div class="formula-display">
                $$ V = \frac{W}{q} $$
            </div>

            <div class="variable-legend">
                <div class="variable-item"><code>V</code> Electric Potential</div>
                <div class="variable-item"><code>W</code> Work Done</div>
                <div class="variable-item"><code>q</code> Charge</div>
            </div>

            <div class="formula-actions">
                <button class="btn btn-outline" onclick="alert('Electric potential is the work done per unit charge. It tells us how much energy is required to move a charge to a specific location.')">Explain Formula</button>
                <button class="btn btn-primary" onclick="alert('Opening Formula Lab: Visualize the energy required to move a charge in a field.')">Open Formula Lab</button>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Card: Key Concepts / Comparison -->
    <div class="card">
        <div class="card-header">
            <span class="card-icon">⚖️</span>
            <h2>Comparison: Field vs. Potential</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="card-body">
            <div class="table-wrapper">
                <table>
                    <thead>
                        <tr>
                            <th>Feature</th>
                            <th>Electric Field (\vec{E})</th>
                            <th>Electric Potential (V)</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Quantity Type</strong></td>
                            <td>Vector (Magnitude + Direction)</td>
                            <td>Scalar (Magnitude only)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>SI Unit</strong></td>
                            <td>Newton per Coulomb (N/C) or Volt/meter (V/m)</td>
                            <td>Volt (V) or Joule per Coulomb (J/C)</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Physical Meaning</strong></td>
                            <td>Force per unit charge</td>
                            <td>Work done per unit charge</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Formula</strong></td>
                            <td>\( \vec{E} = -\nabla V \) (Negative gradient of potential)</td>
                            <td>\( V = -\int \vec{E} \cdot d\vec{l} \) (Line integral of field)</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>

